					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607374103" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1434882050 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Jun 2015 10:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2321422">
									

<p>I enjoyed this, <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">but am no-voting for now.</span></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">My&nbsp;current&nbsp;issues:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">My&nbsp;current&nbsp;issues:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>If any of them are observed communicating with any entity outside the Foundation, all of them are to be terminated, due to the fact that the entity the members of SCP-2422 refer to as “Wandering Father” may pose a security threat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems pretty extreme. I understand after reading the article the risk posed because they were deliberately inserted into the Foundation, but that's still a pretty extreme reaction. Also, is the containment area out in the open? Is it under a Faraday cage or continuous jamming? I'd like to see some evidence of prevention before the Foundation jumps to execution orders. I mean, they don't want to be forced to kill SCP objects because of a flaw in their own procedure.</p>
<p>- The language in the containment and initial description involving &quot;claims&quot;, &quot;resemblances&quot;, and &quot;similar appearances&quot; is way overboard. I get that they're (potentially) not humans, but even for clinical tone, the number of conditional statements is far too high. I nearly stopped reading the article in the second paragraph of the description, and would probably have downvoted there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&lt;Log 2&gt; &quot;<strong>Dr. Welsch:</strong> I’m not– crap.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems like a break in clinical tone. I understand that it's supposed to be a natural slip-up, but it doesn't feel natural to me, so it looks kind of silly in text.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They're called SCP-2422. I know you prefer to designate your own numbers for the entities in your custody, but I'm afraid I must be rather insistent about this one.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is it just because they're recommendation 22? And is there any particular reason the Foundation would go along with this, especially when they believe that these &quot;people&quot; are a security risk?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All personnel who come into contact with SCP-2422-C are to be reminded not to allow it access to any electronic device.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This kind of seems like either a revision to the containment procedures or common sense/standard practice. I know that a reminder is appropriate in the circumstances, but this also gets to the point about prevention planning.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>I might have expected that kind of shit from a D-Class but never one of our researchers. I am disgusted and ashamed. –Dr. Welsch</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Addendum 2422-2 works fine, and implies this without the note. I understand Welsch's response, but this is neither the kind of thing that would stick around in an official document nor something that is furthering your narrative beyond what is already there.<br />
<br /></p>
<p>Edit: Also, the bit with -B threatening the guard. I understand that this was a good way to utilize her ability, but it's kind of melodramatic (Oh no, they're cheating!), and points again to the Foundation not really thinking things through here. I tend to think that if you walk up to a Site and show some level of awareness of the Foundation, you get apprehended, questioned, amnesticized, and released at the bare minimum. Edit edit: MrWrong beat me to it :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>So, overall! The early language almost made me give up entirely. I really like the (pseudo)human drama of the entities, but the Foundation response seems rather wooden (not a huge problem) and their containment procedures a bit&#8230; non-comprehensive. Even a sentence or two about communication jamming or denying electronic equipment would help there.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">As I said, no vote for now.</span> Edit edit edit: Changed to upvote :)</p>

							</div>

								
