					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607374103" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1436422265 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Jul 2015 06:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2334614">
									

<p>It's an issue that's come up across the site before, and one I'm very sympathetic with. Tim Scriven, who has professional experience in the area, has written a very solid post about it here: <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/forum/t-970926/scp-s-and-iq-bonus-discussion-on-personality-tests-as-well">SCP's and IQ (Bonus discussion on personality tests as we</a>.</p>
<p>I'm also a graduate student in psychology by trade. Nowadays, intelligence tests are made up of a bunch of subscales investigating various proposed aspects of intelligence, and theoreticians have to be very careful when talking about intelligence to define exactly what they mean and demonstrate that they aren't just measuring other factors. Modern test development is incredibly difficult (especially as we try to eliminate bias and clarify our theories). It's dicey, but it's important work, and we're trying.</p>
<p>If you wanted this to have a bit more modern scientific validity, I'd check out the description of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wechsler_Adult_Intelligence_Scale">WAIS-IV</a> here. It's one of the broadest used modern tests, and it would give you an idea of the components involved. You could say something like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2422-A: When a human subject establishes direct eye contact with SCP-2422-A, it will gain understanding of the subject’s intelligence level, whether or not it has spoken or interacted with the subject at all. It rates the intelligence of subjects on a scale of 0-35, with the majority of tested subjects falling within 0.1 units of 22. These designations have been found to correlate significantly with most modern measures of intelligence.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-540835-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-540835-1')">1</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>(Or whatever you want to call the Foundation test, if you want one. I just pulled that one out of my ass.)</p>
<p>If you wanted to get a bit more specific/weird with it, you could pick some of the smaller subscales of the WAIS-IV instead of the broader PIQ and VIQ summaries to imply that he's actually only measuring <em>very</em> specific things. That's probably getting out into the weeds, but you could PM me if you like.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-540835-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-540835-1')">1</a>. e.g., Performance IQ and Verbal IQ ratings of the WAIS-IV, Fluid and Crystalized Intelligence Subscales of the Foundation Personnel Aptitude Test.</div>
</div>

