															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607374103" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1436911974 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Jul 2015 22:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2338075">
									

<p>First, I'd like to point out that I like how the supplemental materials are structured in this one. Too many times, the article takes a break from the overall narrative to go into some detailed background document or a collapsible or what have you, and it feels like work to have to slog through the article, constantly going back and forth between different parts of the story and severely disrupting pacing. The way you've done this makes for a better reading experience. So that worked well.</p>
<p>The narrative content, however, has profound issues. For starters, there's this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As a whole, SCP-2422 behaves and interacts similarly to an average middle-class suburban family, with typical alternating congenial and tense internal dynamics observed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I get that this is supposed to be designed as some sort of avatar for the family experience as envisioned by American media (more on that later on), but there is absolutely no such thing as the &quot;average middle-class suburban family&quot; to the point that you can make scientific assessments about behavioral norms and dynamics. This may come across as somewhat of a nitpick, but it represents both the in-universe Foundation and the out-of-universe author having a fallacious view of what constitutes a family in real life, which misinforms and weakens the rest of the article. To put it more succinctly, you're kneecapping your sense of truth with this mindset, which keeps this from having any sort of thematic resonance.</p>
<p>This reliance on trope and stereotype continues with the focal point of your article, the teenaged daughter. Not only is she &quot;depressed teenager&quot; straight from central casting (complete with being molested for some reason), she is of course given empathy powers and emotion-sensing because of course teenage girl. I could have overlooked that, perhaps, in a different article, but again, the &quot;average family&quot; thing up front has already set the table.</p>
<p>&quot;But they're <em>supposed</em> to be stereotypes&quot; is the inevitable response, but the fact that the narrative interest is supposed to come from the fact that they in fact are not stereotypes and are not happy themselves is supposed to be some sort of surprise. That's only a surprise if you originally bought into the view that you espouse above about &quot;average families.&quot; Couple that with yet another use of the Poor Girl in Containment archetype, its own obnoxious stereotype around here, and you've effectively robbed yourself of any ability to rise above cloying, shallow emotional reactions. It's the difference between finding something tragic, and finding it whiny and annoying.</p>
<p>The technical skill with which this was executed is evidently enough to ensure survival, but in my view, the narrative here is completely deficient.</p>

							</div>

								
